Use the **Composite Graph API** (`/composite/graph`), because it meets all three requirements in one call:

- **One API call, one unit of work per order.** Each order is a separate graph in the same request, with `graphId` set to the order number. The graphs are listed in the required order.
- **All or nothing within an order.** Every graph is its own transaction. If any node fails, the whole graph rolls back, so you never get an account without its opportunity or the reverse.
- **Orders are isolated from each other.** One graph failing does not affect the other graphs in the request. Each graph's result reports `isSuccessful` and per-node responses keyed by the referenceIds.

How each node is built:

- **Account upsert.** It uses `PATCH /sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/{value}`. The external ID value goes only in the URL; it must not be repeated in the body. The upsert returns the record `id` whether it creates a new account (201) or updates an existing one (200). The opportunity can therefore reference `@{acct_xxxx.id}` in both cases.
- **Opportunity.** It is created with `AccountId` set to the reference to its own graph's account upsert result.

```http
