**Design choices:**
- **After events only:** the work runs in the after events because inserted records only have ids then.
- **Update rule:** an update qualifies only when the old stage differs from `Closed Won` and the new stage equals it. Edits to an opportunity that was already won are therefore ignored.
- **Service lookup:** the service is fetched through `Application.Service.newInstance`, so `Application.Service.setMock` works in tests.
- **Bulk-safe:** the code is one loop with no SOQL or DML of its own, and makes a single service call per trigger invocation.
- **Sharing:** the domain uses `inherited sharing`, so it runs with the sharing of whatever context calls it.
- **Trigger:** it covers all seven events and only calls `fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler`. That handler finds `Opportunities.Constructor` by name.

**Known limitation:** I did not add a static guard against the same record being processed twice in one transaction. That could happen if a workflow field update re-fires the update trigger, because `Trigger.old` keeps its original values. A guard would also wrongly block a record that is legitimately won, reopened and won again in the same transaction, so I left it out.

Defaults are applied in `onApplyDefaults()`. The library calls this method only from its before-insert handler, so the values are set when records are created and never on update. Each default is written only when the field is still blank, so values the user already set are kept. The trigger only hands off to the domain class.

