/**
 * Service that escalates Cases and creates a follow-up Task for each one it escalates.
 *
 * The selector and the unit of work are obtained only through the Application factories
 * (CasesSelector.newInstance() and Application.UnitOfWork.newInstance()).
 * This lets unit tests replace both with ApexMocks via Application.Selector.setMock
 * and Application.UnitOfWork.setMock.
 */
public with sharing class CaseEscalationService {

    @TestVisible
    private static final String STATUS_CLOSED = 'Closed';
    @TestVisible
    private static final String PRIORITY_HIGH = 'High';
    @TestVisible
    private static final String TASK_SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Escalated: ';

    /**
     * Thrown when escalation is requested with invalid input.
     */
    public class EscalationException extends Exception {}

    /**
     * Escalates the given cases.
     * Closed cases and cases that are already escalated are skipped.
     * Every other case is marked as escalated with High priority, and one Task is created for it.
     * All changes are committed in a single unit of work.
     *
     * @param caseIds Ids of the cases to escalate. A null or empty set is a no-op.
     * @param reason  Escalation reason, used in the Task subject. It must not be blank.
     */
    public static void escalate(Set<Id> caseIds, String reason) {
        // Validate before any read or write.
        if (String.isBlank(reason)) {
            throw new EscalationException('An escalation reason is required.');
        }

        if (caseIds == null || caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Case> cases = CasesSelector.newInstance().selectById(caseIds);
        if (cases == null || cases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        fflib_ISObjectUnitOfWork uow = Application.UnitOfWork.newInstance();
        Date tomorrow = Date.today().addDays(1);
        Boolean hasWork = false;

        for (Case c : cases) {
            if (c == null || !needsEscalation(c)) {
                continue;
            }

            c.IsEscalated = true;
            c.Priority = PRIORITY_HIGH;
            uow.registerDirty(c);

            Task followUp = new Task(
                Subject = TASK_SUBJECT_PREFIX + reason,
                WhatId = c.Id,
                OwnerId = c.OwnerId,
                ActivityDate = tomorrow,
                Priority = PRIORITY_HIGH
            );
            uow.registerNew(followUp);

            hasWork = true;
        }

        if (hasWork) {
            uow.commitWork();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Returns true if the case is not Closed and not already escalated.
     */
    private static Boolean needsEscalation(Case c) {
        if (c.Status == STATUS_CLOSED) {
            return false;
        }
        if (c.IsEscalated == true) {
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

A few design notes:

- **Selected records are updated in place.** The cases returned by the selector are changed directly and passed to `registerDirty`. This keeps one record instance per case, so ApexMocks verifications (same instance or `sObjectWith` matchers) work as expected.
- **Single commit.** The `hasWork` flag makes sure `commitWork()` is called exactly once. It is not called at all when no case qualifies.
- **No hidden dependencies.** The service gets the selector and the unit of work only through the factory calls in the task, so the team's mocks take effect.
- **Sharing.** The class is declared `with sharing`, and the selector is `inherited sharing`, so queries respect the running user's access.

The facade looks up the implementation through the `Application` service factory on every call. This means a mock registered with `Application.Service.setMock` in a test is picked up straight away. Callers only depend on `IOpportunitiesService`, never on `OpportunitiesServiceImpl`.

