/**
 * Service that creates Accounts together with their Contacts using the
 * fflib Unit of Work pattern (single commit, bulkified DML, automatic
 * parent/child relationship resolution).
 */
public with sharing class AccountOnboardingService {

    /**
     * Creates one Account per map key and, for every last name in the key's list,
     * one Contact related to that Account. Everything is committed through a single
     * fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork.
     *
     * @param contactLastNamesByAccountName account name => list of contact last names
     * @return the newly created Accounts with Ids populated (empty list for null/empty input)
     */
    public static List<Account> createAccountsWithContacts(Map<String, List<String>> contactLastNamesByAccountName) {
        List<Account> accounts = new List<Account>();

        if (contactLastNamesByAccountName == null || contactLastNamesByAccountName.isEmpty()) {
            return accounts;
        }

        // Parent types must be listed before child types so the UoW inserts in the right order
        fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork uow = new fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork(
            new List<Schema.SObjectType>{
                Account.SObjectType,
                Contact.SObjectType
            }
        );

        for (String accountName : contactLastNamesByAccountName.keySet()) {
            Account acc = new Account(Name = accountName);
            uow.registerNew(acc);
            accounts.add(acc);

            List<String> lastNames = contactLastNamesByAccountName.get(accountName);
            if (lastNames == null) {
                continue;
            }
            for (String lastName : lastNames) {
                Contact con = new Contact(LastName = lastName);
                // UoW resolves AccountId after the Account has been inserted
                uow.registerNew(con, Contact.AccountId, acc);
            }
        }

        // One bulk insert per SObjectType, wrapped in a savepoint by the UoW
        uow.commitWork();

        // Ids are populated on the same in-memory instances by the UoW
        return accounts;
    }
}
```

I used fflib's `registerPublishAfterSuccessTransaction`, which handles the "publish only after a successful save" rule for you.

**How the save-then-publish rule is met**
- `commitWork()` runs the opportunity updates inside a savepoint.
- The registered `Deal_Won__e` events are published only after that work finishes without error.
- If the update fails, the unit of work rolls back and publishes only "after failure" events, of which none are registered. The exception is then re-thrown to the caller, so Fulfilment never hears about an unsaved deal.
- `Publish Immediately` is the right setting here. The unit of work controls when `EventBus.publish` is called, so the event can't be undone by a later rollback of the commit itself.

**Why `Application` has to change**
- The unit of work only accepts records whose SObject type was registered with the factory. `Deal_Won__e` is not in the list today.
- Without it, `registerPublishAfterSuccessTransaction` throws "SObject type Deal_Won__e is not supported by this unit of work".
- I added it at the end of the list, so the DML order for Account and Opportunity stays the same.

**Choices in the service**
- Only `Id`, `StageName` and `CloseDate` are sent in the update. This avoids rewriting the other selected fields from possibly stale values.
- The class runs `with sharing`.
- `Amount__c` is null when the opportunity has no Amount.

