/**
 * Sets Case.Priority to 'High' when the case's account has Rating = 'Hot'.
 * Every other case keeps the Priority it was created with, including cases with no account.
 *
 * Called from CaseTrigger (before insert). Because this runs before insert,
 * changing a field on the case is enough. No DML is needed.
 *
 * Declared "without sharing" on purpose. The rule must apply no matter who
 * creates the case: the Email-to-Case automated user or the ERP integration
 * user may not have sharing access to every account. The class only reads
 * Account.Rating to decide the priority. It never shows account data to the user.
 */
public without sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    public static final String HOT_RATING = 'Hot';
    public static final String HIGH_PRIORITY = 'High';

    public static void setPriorityFromAccountRating(List<Case> newCases) {
        // Step 1: collect the account Ids from the new cases (skip cases with no account).
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Step 2: ONE query for all the accounts, outside the loop.
        // (A query inside the loop would fail for batches of more than 100 cases.)
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        // Step 3: loop over the cases and look up each case's account in the map.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId == null) {
                continue; // No account: keep the original Priority.
            }
            Account acc = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
            if (acc != null && acc.Rating == HOT_RATING) {
                c.Priority = HIGH_PRIORITY;
            }
            // Otherwise: keep the Priority the case was created with.
        }
    }
}
```

**What I changed and why**

1. **The account query runs once, before the loop, not once per case.**
   - Salesforce allows at most 100 SOQL queries per transaction.
   - Your ERP integration inserts up to 200 cases in one transaction. A query inside the loop would run 200 times.
   - That would throw `System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101`, and the whole batch would fail. That includes any other triggers or flows sharing the same transaction.
   - Email-to-Case would seem fine because it sends one case at a time, so the problem would only appear with the integration.
   - The handler still reads in three simple steps: collect the account Ids, query them once, then loop over the cases and look each account up in a map. One query handles 1 case or 200 cases the same way.

2. **The class is explicitly `without sharing` (my assumption).**
   - The Email-to-Case automated user or the integration user might not have sharing access to the related accounts.
   - If they can't see an account, the Hot rule would silently not apply.
   - The class only reads `Rating` to set a priority, so bypassing sharing here exposes nothing.
   - If you would rather respect the running user's sharing, change it to `with sharing`.

Everything else is as you asked:
- It is a before insert trigger that delegates to the handler.
- Hot accounts set the case `Priority` to `High`.
- All other cases keep the priority they were created with, including cases with no account or whose account is not Hot.

I deviated from the ticket in two ways:

1. **No callout directly from the trigger.** Apex does not allow a synchronous callout from a trigger while the transaction has uncommitted work. The `after insert` trigger always has the new accounts uncommitted, so the callout throws a `CalloutException`. The trigger instead enqueues a Queueable that implements `Database.AllowsCallouts`. It runs right after the insert commits, so the ERP still gets the data within seconds. As a side effect, the user's save no longer waits on the ERP.

2. **One request per trigger chunk, not one per account.** A transaction is limited to 100 callouts. A marketplace batch of 200 accounts would fail at account 101 and roll back the whole insert. The ERP API already accepts an array of 1 to 200 customers, and Salesforce runs triggers in chunks of at most 200 records. So each trigger chunk becomes exactly one request. Every customer in the payload still carries its own `salesforceId`, so the ERP team can trace each account individually. A user creating a single account still produces a request with exactly one customer.

Other design points:
- The job re-queries the accounts by Id, so it sends the data as committed.
- It sends `country: null` explicitly when `BillingCountry` is blank.
- The job splits its Ids into 200-record requests as a safeguard.

**Limitation:** if accounts are inserted from code that is already asynchronous (a Queueable, a Batch `execute`, or a future method), Salesforce allows only one `System.enqueueJob` per transaction. The service checks that limit and skips the sync with a debug message instead of failing the user's DML. If that case matters, a retry or catch-up mechanism would be the follow-up, which this ticket puts out of scope.

