/**
 * Pushes newly created Accounts to the ERP as customers.
 *
 * Callouts are not allowed directly from a trigger (uncommitted work), so the
 * trigger enqueues this Queueable, which performs the callout after commit.
 * The ERP endpoint accepts 1..200 customers per request; trigger chunks are
 * at most 200 records, so each trigger chunk results in exactly one request.
 */
public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    @TestVisible
    private static final Integer MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST = 200;
    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';

    private final List<Id> accountIds;

    public ErpCustomerSync(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = new List<Id>(accountIds);
    }

    /**
     * Entry point used by the trigger.
     */
    public static void enqueue(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        if (Limits.getQueueableJobs() >= Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()) {
            // Avoid failing the user's/integration's DML when the async limit is exhausted
            // (e.g. inserts performed from an already asynchronous context).
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                'ErpCustomerSync: queueable limit reached, ERP sync skipped for ' + accountIds);
            return;
        }
        System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(accountIds));
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
            WITH SYSTEM_MODE
        ];

        List<Customer> batch = new List<Customer>();
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            batch.add(new Customer(acc));
            if (batch.size() == MAX_CUSTOMERS_PER_REQUEST) {
                send(batch);
                batch = new List<Customer>();
            }
        }
        if (!batch.isEmpty()) {
            send(batch);
        }
    }

    private static void send(List<Customer> customers) {
        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
        req.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        req.setMethod('POST');
        req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        req.setTimeout(120000);
        req.setBody(JSON.serialize(customers, false));

        HttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);
        if (res.getStatusCode() != 202) {
            // Error handling / retries are out of scope; log for visibility.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'ErpCustomerSync: ERP responded ' + res.getStatusCode() + ' ' + res.getBody());
        }
    }

    @TestVisible
    private class Customer {
        public String salesforceId;
        public String name;
        public String country;

        Customer(Account acc) {
            this.salesforceId = String.valueOf(acc.Id); // 18-character Id
            this.name = acc.Name;
            this.country = String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? null : acc.BillingCountry;
        }
    }
}
```

I deviated from your spec in one place: the class does not call `EventBus.publish` inside the loop. It builds all the events first, then publishes them with one call to `EventBus.publish(List<SObject>)`.

**Why I changed it:** each `EventBus.publish` call counts as one DML statement, and Apex allows at most 150 DML statements per transaction. Your trigger passes up to 200 opportunities at a time. Publishing inside the loop would throw `System.LimitException: Too many DML statements: 151` on a full batch. It would also use up the DML budget that the rest of the trigger transaction needs.

**You still know which event failed.** `EventBus.publish(List<SObject>)` returns one `Database.SaveResult` per event, in the same order as the input list. Result `i` belongs to `opportunities[i]`. A failure on one event does not stop the others from being published.

Other points:
- **Empty or null input** returns an empty list and does not publish anything. No DML statement is used.
- **Opportunity Id:** `String.valueOf(Id)` always gives the 18-character form. Call this method from an **after** trigger context, because in a before-insert trigger the opportunity Id is still null.
- **Publish behaviour:** because the event is configured as "Publish After Commit", the events are only delivered if the transaction commits. If the transaction rolls back, nothing is sent to the warehouse.
- **`inherited sharing`:** the class runs with the sharing mode of whatever calls it, which is the safe default for a utility class.

