/**
 * Creates follow-up Tasks for opportunities coming out of the weekly pipeline review.
 *
 * Uses a single partial-success DML (Database.insert with allOrNone = false) so that
 * Task validation-rule rejections only affect the individual record, while staying well
 * within the 150-DML-statement governor limit regardless of batch size.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    @TestVisible
    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Follow up: ';
    @TestVisible
    private static final Integer DAYS_UNTIL_DUE = 7;
    @TestVisible
    private static final String TASK_STATUS = 'Not Started';
    @TestVisible
    private static final String TASK_PRIORITY = 'Normal';

    /**
     * Creates one follow-up Task per opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities to create follow-ups for.
     * @return Ids of the opportunities whose Task could not be created
     *         (empty set when every Task was created).
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls.
        Set<Id> requestedIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);
        requestedIds.remove(null);
        if (requestedIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunities = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [
                SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
                FROM Opportunity
                WHERE Id IN :requestedIds
                WITH USER_MODE
            ]
        );

        // Opportunities that could not be found or accessed cannot get a task.
        for (Id requestedId : requestedIds) {
            if (!opportunities.containsKey(requestedId)) {
                failedIds.add(requestedId);
            }
        }

        if (opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(DAYS_UNTIL_DUE);
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        List<Id> taskOpportunityIds = new List<Id>(); // parallel list: index -> opportunity Id

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status = TASK_STATUS,
                Priority = TASK_PRIORITY
            ));
            taskOpportunityIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        // Single DML statement; allOrNone = false so rejected tasks do not block the rest.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results =
            Database.insert(tasks, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);

        // SaveResults are returned in the same order as the input list.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedIds.add(taskOpportunityIds[i]);
            }
        }

        return failedIds;
    }
}
```

I've built this, but I did not use one future call per opportunity. The trigger collects the account IDs into a set and makes one `@future` call per trigger execution.

**Why:** a transaction can make at most 50 `@future` calls. Your CPQ integration saves 200 opportunities at a time, so one call per opportunity would fail on the 51st call. The whole save would then roll back, including the opportunities. Passing the IDs as a set is also bulk-safe because several opportunities on the same account produce only one recalculation.

**Other changes:**

1. **More events trigger a recalculation.** Changing an opportunity's `AccountId` recalculates both the old and the new account. Deleting or undeleting an opportunity also recalculates its account. Without this, ratings would go out of date. Closed won/lost is detected through `StageName`, which you already listed.
2. **A fallback when async isn't allowed.** Apex can't make a future call from code that is already running as a future or batch job. That could happen if an integration runs as a batch job. It also can't make one once the 50-call limit is used up. In those cases the service runs the same logic straight away instead of throwing an error.
3. **Ratings use all opportunities.** The service runs `without sharing`, so ratings depend on all of an account's open opportunities. Otherwise they would depend only on the opportunities visible to the user who saved one. It changes only the `Rating` field.
4. **Efficient queries and writes.** One aggregate query gets the largest open `Amount` per account. Only accounts whose rating actually changes are updated. That update uses partial success, so one bad account doesn't block the others, and failures are written to the debug log.
5. **Assumptions:**
   - An opportunity with no `Amount` counts as an open opportunity under 100,000, so the account is `Warm`.
   - Amounts are compared as stored. In a multi-currency org, the comparison does not convert currencies.

