```apex
/**
 * Keeps Account.Rating in sync with the account's open pipeline.
 *   Hot  - at least one open opportunity with Amount >= 100,000
 *   Warm - open opportunities exist, none that big
 *   Cold - no open opportunities
 *
 * Runs without sharing on purpose: the rating must reflect all of an account's
 * open opportunities, not only those visible to the user who saved one.
 */
public without sharing class AccountRatingService {

    public static final Decimal HOT_THRESHOLD = 100000;
    public static final String RATING_HOT = 'Hot';
    public static final String RATING_WARM = 'Warm';
    public static final String RATING_COLD = 'Cold';

    /**
     * Entry point. Recalculates asynchronously when possible; falls back to
     * synchronous processing where @future cannot be called (already in a
     * future/batch context, or the future-call limit is exhausted).
     */
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Set<Id> ids = sanitize(accountIds);
        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        Boolean canEnqueueFuture =
            !System.isFuture() &&
            !System.isBatch() &&
            Limits.getFutureCalls() < Limits.getLimitFutureCalls();

        if (canEnqueueFuture) {
            recalculateFuture(ids);
        } else {
            recalculateNow(ids);
        }
    }

    @future
    private static void recalculateFuture(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        recalculateNow(accountIds);
    }

    /**
     * Synchronous recalculation. Public so it can be reused (e.g. from a batch
     * or a data-fix script) and tested directly.
     */
    public static void recalculateNow(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Set<Id> ids = sanitize(accountIds);
        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Largest open Amount per account; accounts absent from the result have no open opps
        Map<Id, Decimal> maxOpenAmountByAccount = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
        Set<Id> accountsWithOpenOpps = new Set<Id>();
        for (AggregateResult ar : [
            SELECT AccountId accId, MAX(Amount) maxAmount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :ids AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            Id accId = (Id) ar.get('accId');
            accountsWithOpenOpps.add(accId);
            maxOpenAmountByAccount.put(accId, (Decimal) ar.get('maxAmount'));
        }

        List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Account acc : [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :ids]) {
            String newRating = determineRating(
                accountsWithOpenOpps.contains(acc.Id),
                maxOpenAmountByAccount.get(acc.Id)
            );
            if (acc.Rating != newRating) {
                toUpdate.add(new Account(Id = acc.Id, Rating = newRating));
            }
        }

        if (toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'AccountRatingService: failed to update Account ' + toUpdate[i].Id +
                        ': ' + err.getStatusCode() + ' - ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @TestVisible
    private static String determineRating(Boolean hasOpenOpps, Decimal maxOpenAmount) {
        if (!hasOpenOpps) {
            return RATING_COLD;
        }
        if (maxOpenAmount != null && maxOpenAmount >= HOT_THRESHOLD) {
            return RATING_HOT;
        }
        return RATING_WARM;
    }

    private static Set<Id> sanitize(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Set<Id> ids = new Set<Id>();
        if (accountIds != null) {
            ids.addAll(accountIds);
            ids.remove(null);
        }
        return ids;
    }
}
```

**What I changed:** I didn't call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` once per line. I query the product families once for the whole batch and then call `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` for each line.

**Why:**

1. **Calling it per line would break CPQ quotes.** `standardDiscountFor` runs a SOQL query every time it's called. A 200-line quote would run 200 queries in one transaction, and the synchronous limit is 100. The CPQ insert would fail with `System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101`. The limit is also shared with any other triggers or flows on the same save, so it can fail well below 200 lines.

2. **The rules still live only in `PricingRules`.** `standardDiscountForFamily` is a public method on the pricing team's class, and it is where the family-to-discount mapping actually lives. I only do the product-to-family lookup, in bulk: one query on `Product2` (or one on `PricebookEntry`). I don't modify `PricingRules` or copy its rules, so any change the pricing team makes is picked up automatically.

**Other details:**

- **Only empty discounts are filled.** Lines where `Discount == null` get the standard discount. Lines with an explicit value, including `0`, are left alone.
- **`Product2Id` may be missing.** When a line arrives with only a `PricebookEntryId`, `Product2Id` may not be filled in yet during before insert. In that case the handler gets the family through the `PricebookEntry`.
- **Unresolvable products are skipped.** If a line's product can't be found, the handler leaves `Discount` unchanged. The original approach would throw an exception and fail the whole batch.
- **Sharing is the same as `PricingRules`.** The handler runs `with sharing` and queries `Family` the same way `PricingRules` does, so behaviour doesn't change.

