/**
 * Applies the standard product discount to new opportunity line items
 * that don't already have a Discount. Discount rules are owned by
 * PricingRules; this class only resolves each line's product family in bulk
 * (one query per batch) and delegates the discount decision to
 * PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily.
 */
public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        List<OpportunityLineItem> linesToDefault = new List<OpportunityLineItem>();
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        Set<Id> pricebookEntryIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            // Only default when the rep left Discount empty; 0 is an explicit value.
            if (line.Discount != null) {
                continue;
            }
            linesToDefault.add(line);
            if (line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            } else if (line.PricebookEntryId != null) {
                pricebookEntryIds.add(line.PricebookEntryId);
            }
        }

        if (linesToDefault.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Product -> Family, resolved with at most one query per source.
        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>();
        Set<Id> resolvedProductIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (!productIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (Product2 product : [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]) {
                familyByProductId.put(product.Id, product.Family);
                resolvedProductIds.add(product.Id);
            }
        }

        Map<Id, Id> productIdByPricebookEntryId = new Map<Id, Id>();
        if (!pricebookEntryIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (PricebookEntry entry : [
                SELECT Id, Product2Id, Product2.Family
                FROM PricebookEntry
                WHERE Id IN :pricebookEntryIds
            ]) {
                productIdByPricebookEntryId.put(entry.Id, entry.Product2Id);
                if (entry.Product2Id != null) {
                    familyByProductId.put(entry.Product2Id, entry.Product2.Family);
                    resolvedProductIds.add(entry.Product2Id);
                }
            }
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : linesToDefault) {
            Id productId = line.Product2Id != null
                ? line.Product2Id
                : productIdByPricebookEntryId.get(line.PricebookEntryId);

            if (productId == null || !resolvedProductIds.contains(productId)) {
                // Product couldn't be resolved; leave Discount untouched.
                continue;
            }

            line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(familyByProductId.get(productId));
        }
    }
}
```

I'd use a map instead of the nested loop. The output is the same, but the cost is very different.

**Why not the nested loop:** With 3,000 leads and 3,000 contacts, the nested loop runs up to 9 million comparisons, and each one trims and lowercases a string. The worst case is common here: most event leads won't be existing contacts, so each one scans the whole contact list. Apex enforces a 10-second CPU limit for synchronous transactions, and this runs inside the import transaction. That transaction's CPU time is also shared with triggers, flows and validation on the inserted records. A 9-million-step string loop can easily hit `System.LimitException: Apex CPU time limit exceeded`, and that limit can't be caught, so the whole import would fail.

**What I do instead:**
- Normalise each contact email once (trim and lowercase) and store it in a `Map<String, Id>`.
- Only add an email if it isn't already in the map. This keeps the first contact in `contacts` order, as required.
- Look up each lead's email in the map.

This takes about 6,000 steps instead of 9 million. It's still easy to read, makes no queries or DML, and gives exactly the same results as the nested loop.

**Edge cases handled:**
- A blank or null email never matches.
- A null `leads` list returns an empty list. A null `contacts` list means nothing matches.
- Null elements in either list are handled without errors.
- Lowercasing uses a fixed locale (`en_US`) so matching doesn't change with the running user's locale. For example, the Turkish dotless "i" would otherwise break some matches.

