The action records the IDs of the Tasks it inserts in a static set, and checks that those Tasks still exist before logging again. This handles the rounding re-entry, and it still produces the audit Task if Salesforce retries the trigger after a partial-success rollback.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/TA_Opportunity_LogAmountChange.cls
```apex
/**
 * @description After-update Trigger Action that creates a completed "Amount changed" Task
 * whenever an Opportunity's Amount changes.
 *
 * Re-entrancy: TA_Opportunity_RoundAmount (Order 20) updates the same opportunities again in
 * the same transaction, which re-runs this action. The audit must create at most one Task
 * per opportunity per transaction.
 *
 * A plain static Set<Id> of "already logged" opportunity Ids is not enough. On a partial-success
 * DML (Database.update(records, false)), Salesforce rolls back and re-runs the triggers for the
 * successful records without resetting static variables. A static set would then skip those
 * records on the retry, and their rolled-back Tasks would be lost.
 *
 * So we remember the Ids of the Tasks we insert, and before logging we query which of them
 * still exist:
 *  - In a recursive execution, the Task inserted earlier in the transaction exists, so we skip.
 *  - After a rollback or retry, the Task no longer exists, so we log again.
 *
 * Bulk-safe: at most one SOQL query and one DML statement per trigger execution.
 */
public with sharing class TA_Opportunity_LogAmountChange implements TriggerAction.AfterUpdate {
    @TestVisible
    private static final String TASK_SUBJECT = 'Amount changed';
    @TestVisible
    private static final String TASK_STATUS = 'Completed';

    /** Ids of audit Tasks inserted by this action during the current transaction. */
    @TestVisible
    private static Set<Id> insertedTaskIds = new Set<Id>();

    public void afterUpdate(List<Opportunity> triggerNew, List<Opportunity> triggerOld) {
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oldMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(triggerOld);

        Set<Id> changedIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : triggerNew) {
            Opportunity oldOpp = oldMap.get(opp.Id);
            if (oldOpp != null && opp.Amount != oldOpp.Amount) {
                changedIds.add(opp.Id);
            }
        }
        if (changedIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        changedIds.removeAll(getAlreadyLoggedOpportunityIds(changedIds));
        if (changedIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opp : triggerNew) {
            if (changedIds.contains(opp.Id)) {
                tasks.add(
                    new Task(
                        Subject = TASK_SUBJECT,
                        WhatId = opp.Id,
                        Status = TASK_STATUS
                    )
                );
            }
        }

        // The audit trail is system-generated, so it is inserted in system mode
        // (the default for Apex DML) regardless of the editing user's Task permissions.
        insert tasks;

        for (Task t : tasks) {
            insertedTaskIds.add(t.Id);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Returns the Ids of the given opportunities that already have an audit Task created by
     * this action in the current transaction and not rolled back.
     */
    private static Set<Id> getAlreadyLoggedOpportunityIds(Set<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> alreadyLogged = new Set<Id>();
        if (insertedTaskIds.isEmpty()) {
            return alreadyLogged;
        }
        for (Task t : [
            SELECT WhatId
            FROM Task
            WHERE Id IN :insertedTaskIds AND WhatId IN :opportunityIds
            WITH SYSTEM_MODE
        ]) {
            alreadyLogged.add(t.WhatId);
        }
        return alreadyLogged;
    }
}
```

